## Menu filter: responding to clicks
Now you build interactivity driven by the *user*: a **menu filter** where clicking a category button (Espresso, Brewed, Cold) shows only the matching items and hides the rest. This is the event-driven model in action, listen for clicks, change what is shown:
```js
const buttons = document.querySelectorAll('.filter-btn');
const items = document.querySelectorAll('.menu-item');

buttons.forEach(btn => {
  btn.addEventListener('click', () => {
    const category = btn.dataset.category;            // e.g. "cold"
    items.forEach(item => {
      const match = category === 'all' || item.dataset.category === category;
      item.classList.toggle('hidden', !match);        // show/hide via a class
    });
  });
});
```
The pattern: attach a click listener to each button, and on click, loop over the items showing or hiding each based on whether it matches, using a `.hidden` CSS class (which sets `display: none`) toggled by JavaScript.

## The key technique: toggle classes, don't set styles directly
The most important practice here is that JavaScript changes appearance by **adding and removing CSS classes**, not by setting individual styles directly. Notice it does `item.classList.toggle('hidden', !match)`, not `item.style.display = 'none'`. This class-toggling approach is the right way, and understanding why matters:
- **Separation of concerns (again):** the *styling* of `.hidden` (or `.active`, or `.dark`) lives in your CSS where styling belongs, and JavaScript only decides *which class applies*, keeping behaviour (JS) and appearance (CSS) separate, the same principle from week one, now in interaction.
- **Maintainability:** if `.hidden` needs to change (a fade animation instead of an instant hide), you edit one CSS rule, not scattered JavaScript, and all the visual logic stays in the stylesheet.
- **Power:** a class can apply many style changes at once (a `.dark` class might restyle dozens of elements), which JavaScript toggling one class triggers cleanly, where setting individual styles would be dozens of JS lines.
So the discipline is: **JavaScript toggles classes; CSS defines what those classes look like.** This single pattern, JS manages state via classes, CSS handles the visuals, is how nearly all interactive styling is done, and it scales from this filter to dark mode (tomorrow) to entire design systems.

## Reading data from the DOM with data attributes
Notice `btn.dataset.category` and `item.dataset.category`, these read custom **data attributes** (`data-category="cold"` in the HTML), the clean way to attach data to elements for JavaScript to use:
```html
<button class="filter-btn" data-category="cold">Cold</button>
<div class="menu-item" data-category="cold">Cold Brew</div>
```
Data attributes let you store information *on* the HTML elements (which category each belongs to) that your JavaScript reads, rather than hard-coding logic or parsing text. This keeps the data tied to the element it describes and the JavaScript generic (it just compares categories, with no knowledge of specific items), so adding a new menu item is just HTML with the right `data-category`, no JavaScript change. This pattern, data attributes carrying element-specific info that generic JS acts on, is a clean, scalable way to build interactive features, and it is exactly the kind of structure that frameworks later formalise. The filter works for any number of items and categories because the data lives in the markup and the logic is generic.

## Where this fits
You built the menu filter, click listeners that show/hide items by toggling CSS classes (JS manages state via classes, CSS defines the look) and reading element data from `data-` attributes. This is the core pattern of click-driven interactivity. Tomorrow you build dark mode, which adds *persistence*, remembering the user's choice across visits.

